  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nZonal upper level flow will continue through Friday bringing much\nabove average temperatures and dry conditions. This will culminate\nFriday when temperatures are expected to be in the 80s F, relative\nhumidity values in the single digits to teens, and wind gusts \nin the 20s and 30s mph. Elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions are expected Friday, especially along the foothills\nwest of Billings where wind gusts in excess of 40 mph are \npossible (60-80%). \n\nA pattern change is expected Saturday through early next week with\ncooler and wetter conditions expected. There are a lot of \nvariables with this system that, combined with how far out it is \n(3-5 days), are making the forecast highly uncertain at this time.\nThese variables include a splitting trough over the Pacific \nNorthwest, with one diving south along the west coast and the \nother moving east along northern CONUS, and hurricane Rachel. \nAnalysis of deterministic models indicates that a low pressure \nsystem associated with the Pacific Northwest trough will slide \neast bringing a cold front and the initial batch of moisture \nSaturday. Hurricane Rachel then weakens and heads towards the \nCalifornia coast Sunday and Monday, eventually combining with a \ncutoff low. This will act to advect additional moisture into \nMontana Sunday and Monday. \n\nIt appears that potential precipitation amounts have increased\neast of Billings due to this additional moisture being brought in\nby Rachel, though the highest amounts are still expected for the\nmountains and foothills. Areas west of a line from Roundup to\nSheridan have a 70-90% chance of getting greater than 0.5 inches\nof precipitation, with areas to the east having a 30-60% chance.\nPrecipitation type is expected to be mostly snow for the mountains\nand foothills and a rain/snow mix for the rest of the region. \nSignificant uncertainty makes it difficult to predict snow amounts\nat this time, but confidence is high that the Absaroka/Beartooth \nand Crazy Mountains will see their first heavy snow of the season,\nwith the foothills west of Billings and the Bighorn Mountains \nseeing at least light snow. Warmer temperatures and warm ground \nsurfaces will make accumulating snow more difficult for lower \nelevations, though even these areas have a 20-50% chance of \ngetting greater than 1 inch. This pattern will likely bring the \nfirst widespread frost of the season Sunday and Monday mornings \nas high temperatures these days are only expected to be in the 40s\nF. Torgerson",
